ration counts / tolerance settings as part of the efficiency metric, not just wall-time and memory.\\n3. Explicitly mark the gradient-agreement check as a diagnostic with tolerance guidance (relative error / cosine similarity), noted as a sanity check on the implementation rather than a pass/fail claim, and define what is reported if tolerance tuning is needed.\\n4. Frame the pure-Python IFT check as an executable design/feasibility element demonstrating the math the TorchOpt API encodes (analytic implicit function, IFT backward vs finite differences), clearly not claimed as evidence that the torch/torchopt pipeline ran.\\n5. Keep clearly separated: what is specified for execution vs. what can actually run in this sandbox (stdlib only) vs. what requires a torch+torchopt environment, and state the runtime/CPU-budget assumption explicitly.\\n\\nThere are no blocking objections; proceed with the deliverable.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
{"id": "807a7116-a6e7-440f-bbc2-9c112c7c574f", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T15:09:01.114025", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "2ace9e9c-0fd6-4527-9059-e88d1446d1db", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "The approach is approved. I'll now produce the complete final experiment design, folding in all five refinements with clear separation of what is executable here vs. specified-only.\n\n"}], "reasoning_content": "The researcher approved the approach and asked me to produce the final experiment design (stage=result) with the refinements folded in. Let me write a comprehensive final design document.\n\nLet me structure the final deliverable:\n\n1. Title & Research Question\n2. Method: TorchOpt implicit differentiation for meta-learning\n   - Setup: bi-level optimization formalization, iMAML\n   - Route 1: custom_root (explicit)\n   - Route 2: F.module(implicit=True) + MetaOptimizer(implicit=True)\n   - How the backward works (IFT math)\n3. Experiment A (executable now, pure-Python IFT sanity check) — fully specified\n4. Experiment B (specified but requires torch+torchopt) — sine-wave few-shot regression\n   - Data\n   - Model\n   - Inner loop (identical across routes/controls)\n   - Implicit route specifics + convergence enforcement (residual/stationarity checks, rtol/atol/maxiter)\n   - Controls (unrolled MAML, first-order MAML, gradient agreement)\n   - Metrics: gradient agreement (with diagnostic framing), few-shot MSE, efficiency (wall-time, memory, CG iterations/tolerance)\n   - Hyperparameters\n5. Analysis plan\n6. Feasibility and environment requirements (what runs here vs. needs torch+torchopt), runtime budget assumption\n7. Assumptions and uncertainties (restate)\n8. Evidence summary\n\nLet me be careful and precise. Since the researcher approved and there are no blocking objections, I should deliver the final design and not invent new objections. I should still be honest that the torch-based experiment is specified-not-executed.\n\nLet me write out the details carefully.\n\nIFT math for the design:\n- Meta-parameters θ (outer), task parameters φ* (inner converged).\n- Inner objective: ℒ_in(φ; θ, D_task^tr)\n- Inner solve: φ*(θ) = argmin_φ ℒ_in(φ; θ, D^tr), equivalently stationarity g(φ, θ) = ∇_φ ℒ_in(φ; θ, D^tr) = 0.\n- Outer objective: ℒ_out(φ*(θ)) = ℒ_out(φ*(θ); D^val).\n- Implicit function theorem: \n  dφ*/dθ = −[∇_φ² ℒ_in]^{-1} ∇_θ ∇_φ ℒ_in  (at the root)\n- dℒ_out/dθ = ∇_φ ℒ_out · dφ*/dθ = −∇_φ ℒ_out · [∇_φ² ℒ_in]^{-1} · ∇_θ ∇_φ ℒ_in\n  Computed as: solve linear system [∇_φ² ℒ_in]^T v = −(∇_φ ℒ_out)^T for v (via CG with HVP), then outer grad = v^T ∇_θ ∇_φ ℒ_in.\n\nThis is exactly what TorchOpt's `implicit.diff`/`custom_root` does: forward = inner solve to fixed point; backward = inverse-HVP linear solve + mixed-partial VJP, using only first-order oracle (HVP) — no second derivative unrolling, O(1) in inner loop depth.\n\nFor Route 1 (custom_root): user provides:\n- `inner_phi = solve(phi0, theta, D_tr)` — a function mapping (phi, theta) -> updated (phi) that is a fixed-point iteration (e.g., K steps of SGD; for exact iMAML, run many steps until convergence). \n- Actually for custom_root, you register function `f(phi, theta)` that returns the residual/iteration; the root is φ*(θ) with f(φ*, θ) = φ*. TorchOpt differentiates through the implicit root.\n\nRoute 2: `torchopt.F.module(net, implicit=True)` + `torchopt.MetaOptimizer(net, torch.optim.SGD, lr=..., implicit=True)`; `meta_opt.step()` performs the update via implicit view of the optimizer's fixed point, and the outer gradient flows through φ*(θ).\n\nConvergence enforcement/diagnosis for implicit route:\n- Run inner SGD with fixed K (identical across routes). For IFT validity, require the inner iteration to be near a fixed point: measure the stationarity residual ‖∇_φ ℒ_in(φ_K; θ, D_tr)‖ (or the per-step update norm ‖φ_K − φ_{K−1}‖) at the end of the inner loop.\n- Diagnostics: report residual trace across a pilot set of (θ, task) pairs; increase K or lower optimizer lr until residual meets a tolerance (e.g., ||∇φ L_in || / (||∇φ L_in at init|| + eps) < 1e-3, or update-norm < atol). Only then is the implicit gradient trusted. Report rtol/atol/maxiter used in the custom_root solve (these are solver tolerances for both the forward outer solve where applicable and the backward inverse-HVP CG).\n- Also state: unrolled and first-order routes use identical K and SAME inner optimizer/step schedule so comparison is clean; the only difference is how the outer gradient is obtained (unrolled graph / detached / implicit root).\n\nCG iteration reporting: For the implicit backward, TorchOpt/its solver runs CG to solve the inverse-HVP system; the design records: number of CG iterations per outer step, final residual norm, chosen rtol/atol/maxiter. Report these alongside wall-time and memory. Compare with unrolled which costs K VJPs.\n\nGradient agreement diagnostic framing:\n- On a small pilot task, compute implicit gradient g_imp, unrolled gradient g_unroll, first-order g_fo, and finite-difference gradient g_fd (for tiny param dims). Report cosine similarity and relative L2 error: cos = ⟨g_imp, g_unroll⟩/(‖g_imp‖‖g_unroll‖), rel_err = ‖g_imp − g_unroll‖/‖g_unroll‖.\n- Framed as a sanity check/diagnostic, NOT binary pass/fail claim. Tolerance guidance: cos > 0.999 and rel_err < 1e-3 for converged inner loop is expected; if divergence, report the curve vs. inner-loop K and residual — the check may indicate insufficient inner convergence rather than an implementation bug. If tolerance tuning needed, report agreement as a function of residual/tolerance rather than fixing a single threshold.\n\nPure-Python IFT check (executable now):\n- Analytic implicit function: e.g., y*(θ) defined by g(y, θ) = y^3 − θ = 0 (real root), or a slightly richer Rosenbrock-style fixed point. Actually pick something with a closed-form + a nontrivial Hessian: g(y, θ) = y^3 − θ; d y*/dθ = 1/(3 y²) = (1/3) θ^{-2/3}. \n- Validate via stdlib-only numeric differentiation of the IFT backward implementation (a stand-in for custom_root's backward): implement linear solve by simple iterative/Gaussian elimination with float arithmetic + finite-difference Hessian (since no autograd), compare to analytic derivative at several θ values, and to finite differences of the root y*(θ).\n- This demonstrates the math (the recipe custom_root encodes) without claiming the torch/torchopt pipeline ran.\n\nEnvironment separation:\n- Can run now (stdlib only, this sandbox): pure-Python IFT sanity check (Experiment A). Also the full design document and code skeletons.\n- Requires torch+torchopt (specified, NOT executable here): Experiment B (sine-wave regression, all routes/controls/metrics). Note Python 3.14 may lack torch wheels; execution environment must provide a compatible python (3.9–3.12 recommended) with torch>=2.x and torchopt (e.g., pip install torchopt, or run from a TorchOpt checkout).\n\nRuntime/CPU budget assumption: CPU-only; 224 cores available in this sandbox but torch unavailable. For Experiment B, assume up to a few hours on a CPU-only machine for the pilot + main run, using small model (MLP 1-40-40-1, ~3.2k params) and meta-batch 4–16 tasks/iter, 20k–50k outer iters; wall-time budget stated as an assumption not a fixed requirement.\n\nLet me now also include exact code skeletons (short, illustrative) for Route 1, Route 2, and the pure-Python check in the design's answer. I'll keep them concise but concrete.\n\nLet me write the final answer. I'll use end_researcher_turn with stage=result.\n\nOne consideration: the answer field should be comprehensive. Let me write it well-structured but not bloated. The researcher asked to \"produce the final experiment design covering rationale, controls, analysis plan, and feasibility\" with the 5 refinements folded in.\n\nLet me draft.", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "FINAL EXPERIMENT DESIGN — \"How can you implement implicit differentiation for meta-learning using TorchOpt?\"\n\n=====================================================================\n0. DESIGN-ONLY STATUS AND GROUNDING\n=====================================================================\nThis is a design-only deliverable (task.json: \"repositories and compute are not required\"; no experiment was run). Inspection confirmed /workspace contains only task.json; no TorchOpt repo, no torch/torchopt/numpy/jax anywhere on disk; Python 3.14.4; network disabled. The design is therefore grounded in TorchOpt's DOCUMENTED public API (flagged \"not on-disk-verified\"), with:\n  • Step 0 (pinning) built into every script: print `torchopt.__version__` and the docstrings/signatures of `torchopt.diff.implicit.custom_root`, `torchopt.diff.implicit.diff`, `torchopt.F.module`, `torchopt.MetaOptimizer` and assert assumptions about keyword names before running; any mismatch is recorded, not silently worked around.\n  • An executable-in-this-sandbox, stdlib-only sanity check (Experiment A) that demonstrates the mathematics the TorchOpt API encodes, WITHOUT claiming the torch/torchopt pipeline ran.\n  • A fully specified torch/torchopt experiment (Experiment B) marked NOT-executable here.\n\n=====================================================================\n1. RATIONALE & RESEARCH QUESTION\n=====================================================================\nQuestion: how to implement implicit differentiation for meta-learning using TorchOpt? Answer = (method recipe) + (validation design).\n\nFormalization (bi-level meta-learning):\n  Outer:   min_θ  E_task ~ p(T) [ ℒ_out( φ*(θ; T) ; D^val_T ) ]\n  Inner:   φ*(θ;T) = argmin_φ ℒ_in( φ ; θ, D^tr_T )   ⇔   g(φ,θ)=∇_φ ℒ_in(φ; θ, D^tr_T)=0  (stationarity)\n\nTwo ways to get the outer gradient dℒ_out/dθ:\n  • Unrolled (MAML): backprop through the K inner steps — memory/time grow with K.\n  • Implicit (iMAML, Rajeswaran et al. 2019): at a CONVERGED inner optimum, by the implicit function theorem,\n        dφ*/dθ = −[∇²_φ ℒ_in]^{-1} · ∇_θ∇_φ ℒ_in\n        dℒ_out/dθ = −(∇_φ ℒ_out)^T · [∇²_φ ℒ_in]^{-1} · ∇_θ∇_φ ℒ_in   (solved via inverse-HVP linear solve, e.g. CG; O(1) in K, needs only first-order HVPs).\n    This is precisely the backward TorchOpt's `diff.implicit` machinery computes: forward = solve to the inner fixed point, backward = linear solve + mixed-partial VJP.\n\nDesign answer structure:\n  (A) Recipe using TorchOpt, two routes; (B) validation: stdlib-only IFT check (executable) + torch-based experiment with controls/metrics (specified).\n\n=====================================================================\n2. METHOD — TORCHOPT RECIPE (two routes)\n=====================================================================\nShared skeleton (functional model + inner loop identical across routes and controls):\n```\nimport torch, torch.nn.functional as F, torchopt\nfrom torchopt import nn as tno\n\nclass SineMLP(tno.Functional):          # functional module, params-first\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__(); self.fc1=tno.Linear(1,40); self.fc2=tno.Linear(40,40); self.fc3=tno.Linear(40,1)\n    def forward(self,x,params=None):\n        if params is None: params=torchopt.extract_state_dict(self, namespace='flatten')\n        h=F.relu(self.fc1(x,params['fc1'])); h=F.relu(self.fc2(h,params['fc2'])); return self.fc3(h,params['fc3'])\n\ndef inner_loss(phi, theta, xs, ys):      # ℒ_in on support set; theta=meta params\n    return F.mse_loss(SineMLP()(xs, phi), ys)   # phi is the current task params\ndef outer_loss(phi, theta, xq, yq):      # ℒ_out on query set\n    return F.mse_loss(SineMLP()(xq, phi), yq)\n```\nROUTE 1 — explicit, most instructive (this is the \"how to implement\" core):\n```\nfrom torchopt import diff\nimplicit_diff = diff.implicit.custom_root(\n    solve=<solver handle, e.g. CG provided by the installed torchopt/linear-solver>,  # pinned in Step 0\n    argnums=0, has_aux=True, rtol=1e-6, atol=1e-8, maxiter=50)\n# Decorate the inner fixed-point iteration so the root φ*(θ) is differentiated implicitly:\n@implicit_diff\ndef inner_fixed_point(phi, theta, xs, ys):     # returns updated phi (root: phi'-phi=0 at convergence)\n    for _ in range(K):\n        g = torch.autograd.grad(inner_loss(phi, theta, xs, ys), phi, create_graph=False)[0]\n        with torch.no_grad(): phi = [p - inner_lr*gp for p,gp in zip(phi,g)]\n    return phi, (residual_info,)               # has_aux returns diagnostics\n# Outer step:\nphi_star, (res,) = inner_fixed_point(meta_theta, meta_theta, x_tr, y_tr)   # forward solve\nouter_loss_val = outer_loss(phi_star, meta_theta, x_qu, y_qu)\nouter_loss_val.backward(); meta_opt.step()     # implicit backward flows via decorator\n```\nROUTE 2 — high-level TorchOpt meta API:\n```\nnet = torchopt.F.module(SineMLP(), implicit=True)\nmeta_opt = torchopt.MetaOptimizer(net, torch.optim.SGD, lr=inner_lr, implicit=True)  # subclass e.g. MetaSGD\n# inner loop: net.zero_grad(); inner_loss.backward(); meta_opt.step()   → implicit update, no unrolled graph\n# outer loop: standard torch.optim.Adam over meta_theta\n```\nBoth routes are specified; Route 1 is the primary answer (maximum control over the root condition and solver), Route 2 demonstrates the convenience API. Exact keyword names (`implicit`, `solve`, `argnums`, `has_aux`) are pinned in Step 0.\n\nInner-loop convergence enforcement & diagnosis (Refinement 1) — CRITICAL because IFT is only valid at a converged inner optimum:\n  • All routes use the SAME inner optimizer (SGD), SAME learning rate inner_lr, SAME K, SAME initialization φ₀=θ (copy) — the only difference is how the outer gradient is computed, so the comparison is clean.\n  • Pre-run convergence check (pilot, 100 tasks): run inner loop and record per-step (a) update norm ‖φₖ−φₖ₋₁‖ and (b) stationarity residual ‖∇_φ ℒ_in(φₖ)‖ (normalized by initial residual). Increase K (or lower inner_lr) until, on ≥95% of pilot tasks, ‖∇_φ ℒ_in(φ_K)‖/‖∇_φ ℒ_in(φ₀)‖ < 1e-3 (or update-norm < atol for the chosen solver). These K and inner_lr values are then FIXED and reused identically by unrolled/first-order/implicit routes.\n  • At runtime, the implicit route ALSO emits residual_info per task (aux output) and logs the residual distribution per outer iteration; any outer step where residual exceeds the threshold is flagged (counted and reported), never silently trusted.\n  • The `rtol/atol/maxiter` args of `custom_root`/solver govern the inner/backward solve tolerances and are reported, not hidden.\n\n=====================================================================\n3. EXPERIMENT A — EXECUTABLE NOW IN THIS SANDBOX (stdlib only)\n=====================================================================\nPurpose: demonstrate the mathematics TorchOpt's implicit diff encodes, with ZERO dependencies, in this sandbox (Python 3.14.4, no torch). Framed explicitly as a math/feasibility check, NOT evidence that the TorchOpt pipeline ran.\nSpecification:\n  • Pick an analytic implicit function: y*(θ) = real root of g(y,θ)=y³−θ=0 ⇒ y*=θ^{1/3}, analytic dy*/dθ = 1/(3θ^{2/3}). (Also run the Rosenbrock-style 2D fixed point x* = φ(x*,θ) for a second case.)\n  • Hand-implement the IFT backward exactly as `custom_root` does: given the root g=0, compute dy*/dθ = −[∂g/∂y]^{-1}·∂g/∂θ using finite-difference partial derivatives (no autograd available) and a tiny dense solve implemented in pure Python.\n  • Verify: (i) against the analytic derivative, and (ii) against central finite differences of the root y*(θ). Report relative error and absolute error at several θ values.\n  • Output: a small table + assertion thresholds like rel-err < 1e-6. Run via `python3 experiment_A_ift.py` — fully runnable here.\nDeliverable form: a single stdlib script plus a report of pass/critical residuals. This is the only executed portion in this sandbox.\n\n=====================================================================\n4. EXPERIMENT B — SPECIFIED, REQUIRES torch + torchopt (NOT executable here)\n=====================================================================\nEnvironment requirements (explicit): CPython 3.9–3.12 recommended (Python 3.14 likely lacks torch wheels), torch ≥ 2.x (needs torch.func/functorch for HVPs TorchOpt's implicit backward uses), torchopt from the pinned version's docs (pip install torchopt or a TorchOpt checkout). Network needed at setup time only; offline afterwards.\nData: fully synthetic sine-wave few-shot regression (Finn et al. 2017 style): each task = y = A·sin(x−φ)+ε, A~U[0.1,5], φ~U[0,π]; support = 10 x-points, query = 10; 1000 meta-train tasks + 100 held-out meta-test tasks (generated by fixed seed, no download — offline-safe).\n\nModel: SineMLP above (≈3.3k params).\n\nExperimental arms (all share the identical inner loop fixed in the pilot):\n  R1 = Explicit implicit (`diff.implicit.custom_root`), K from convergence pilot.\n  R2 = High-level implicit (`F.module(implicit=True)` + `MetaOptimizer(implicit=True)`), same K.\n  C1 = Unrolled MAML control: same inner loop, but `F.module(default)`/unrolled mode — backprop through the K steps.\n  C2 = First-order MAML control: same inner loop with stop-gradient (detach φ) — isolates the value of the implicit term.\n  C0 = Gradient-agreement diagnostic (see metrics).\n\nHyperparameters (fixed defaults; state as assumptions): outer Adam lr 1e-3, inner SGD lr 0.01, meta-batch 8 tasks/step, 20k–50k outer steps for full run (short run 2k steps for the pilot), K tuned in pilot (default 5, raised until residual threshold met), reproducibility: fixed seeds (python/torch/numpy), single config-file defining all arms.\n\nMetrics & analysis plan:\n  M1 Gradient-agreement diagnostic (Refinement 3, framed as a SANITY CHECK not a pass/fail claim): on one small task, compute g_imp (R1), g_unroll (C1), g_fo (C2), and g_fd (central finite differences; feasible at this scale). Report cosine similarity cos=⟨g_imp,g_unroll⟩/(‖·‖‖·‖) and relative L2 error ‖g_imp−g_unroll‖/‖g_unroll‖. Expected at converged inner loop: cos>0.999, rel-err<1e-3. If not met, report agreement as a function of K and of residual (the diagnostic distinguishes \"insufficient inner convergence\" from \"implementation bug\"); no binary claim is made, and the tuning/tolerance path (e.g. raise K, lower inner_lr, loosen/ tighten solver rtol/atol/maxiter) is reported alongside the numbers.\n  M2 Meta-learning quality: outer-loss curves (smoothed), final few-shot test MSE = mean±std over the 100 held-out tasks (R1 vs R2 vs C1 vs C2). Report per-arm and paired differences with error bars across 3 seeds.\n  M3 Efficiency (Refinements 2): per-outer-step wall-clock time, peak memory (torch.cuda/psutil; CPU-only here), AND for the implicit arms the linear-solve cost: number of CG (or chosen solver) iterations per outer step, final residual norm of the inverse-HVP solve, and the solver's rtol/atol/maxiter. Plus the key K-sensitivity claim, explicitly framed as a claim to be tested: sweep K∈{1,5,10,20} and show implicit cost ~flat in K while unrolled cost grows ~linearly — report the fitted slope.\nAnalysis plan summary: (1) run pilot (convergence/agreement/gradient curves), (2) fix K/lr from pilot, (3) run all arms with identical budgets, (4) produce tables: gradient agreement, final MSE, cost-per-step, CG stats, K-sensitivity; (5) report residuals/flag rates for the implicit arms alongside results.\n\n=====================================================================\n5. FEASIBILITY & EXECUTION-SCOPE SEPARATION (Refinement 5)\n=====================================================================\n  • Runs NOW in this sandbox (stdlib only): Experiment A (pure-Python IFT math check) — the guaranteed-executed portion.\n  • SPECIFIED but NOT executable here (requires torch+torchopt env with network at setup): Experiment B (all arms, metrics, analysis) — produced as code skeleton + config + run instructions; no executed results claimed for it.\n  • Runtime budget assumption (CPU-only, 224 cores available in sandbox, no GPU): pilot ≈ 5–15 min; short run ≈ 30–60 min; full run + K-sweep ≈ 2–4 h on a single CPU node. This is an explicit assumption, not a requirement, since the sandbox cannot run torch.\n  • Step-0 pinning is part of Experiment B's precondition: verify torchopt version and docstrings before launch; abort-and-report on signature mismatch rather than guessing.\n\n=====================================================================\n6. ASSUMPTIONS\n=====================================================================\n  - TorchOpt's documented public API (custom_root, diff.implicit, F.module, MetaOptimizer(...,implicit), nn.Functional, extract_state_dict) is authoritative for the method since no on-disk repo/docs exist here and network is disabled; exact signatures pinned at execution.\n  - Implicit differentiation is applied to the OUTER gradient of a bi-level meta-learning objective (iMAML-style); inner loop is ordinary functional SGD, shared verbatim across arms/controls.\n  - The IFT is only used after the inner-loop convergence/residual criteria from the pilot are met; flagged tasks are never silently trusted.\n  - A torch+torchopt environment (CPython 3.9–3.12, torch≥2.x) and network-at-setup are provided at execution time; Python 3.14.4 in this sandbox cannot host them.\n  - Synthetic sine-wave data (no download), CPU-only, and the stated runtime budgets are acceptable for the torch-based run.\n  - Fixed seeds and identical hyperparameters across arms make the comparison clean; the design fixes defaults and reports any tuning as sensitivity analysis, not ad-hoc changes.\n\n=====================================================================\n7. UNCERTAINTIES\n=====================================================================\n  - Exact TorchOpt version API (solver handle location, `implicit` keyword placement, `custom_root` signature) unverifiable offline → Step-0 pinning; design includes fallback naming in the skeleton.\n  - Python 3.14 wheel availability for torch/torchopt at execution time is unknown → Experiment B constrained to 3.9–3.12.\n  - Optimal K / inner_lr for the residual criterion requires the pilot; defaults given.\n  - Gradient-agreement tolerance targets need pilot tuning → reported as diagnostic curves, not binary claims.\n  - Whether the runtime owner chooses to supply a TorchOpt checkout at execution (task.json says not required) → if not, only Experiment A executes; Experiment B remains fully specified-but-unexecuted.\n\nEvidence: exploration results above (ls/cat/find/python checks) confirm: empty /workspace except task.json; task.json declares design-only; no torchopt source, wheel, git repo, docs, or ML packages anywhere; python 3.14.4; no network/pip env. These are the grounded observations behind the design's grounding strategy and execution-scope separation.", "evidence": "Exploratory checks run in the sandbox (all successful, recorded in prior turns): (1) `ls -la /workspace` → only task.json; (2) `cat /workspace/task.json` → documents.track.md = \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"; (3) `find / -iname '*torchopt*'` → empty; archive find → no torchopt wheel/repo, only system archives; no `.git` dirs; no project `.md/.rst`; (4) `python3 -c 'import torch'` → ModuleNotFoundError; importlib.find_spec for numpy/scipy/matplotlib/pandas/jax/functorch/optax all False; (5) python3 = 3.14.4; pip list shows only system packages; no pip index/proxy env vars. These confirm no repo/source/docs/deps/network, grounding the design in documented API with Step-0 pinning, a stdlib-only executable check (Experiment A), and a specified-only torch experiment (Experiment B).", "assumptions": ["TorchOpt's documented public API is authoritative for the method in the absence of on-disk repo/docs and network; exact signatures are pinned against the installed version's docstrings at execution (Step 0).", "Implicit differentiation is used for the OUTER/meta gradient of a bi-level meta-learning objective (iMAML-style); inner loop is ordinary functional SGD shared verbatim across all arms and controls.", "The implicit-function-theorem gradient is only trusted after inner-loop convergence/residual criteria from a pilot are met; non-converged tasks are flagged and reported, never silently included.", "Execution of the torch/torchopt experiment requires a separate environment: CPython 3.9–3.12, torch >= 2.x, and torchopt installed (network at setup only); this sandbox (Python 3.14.4, no torch, no network) cannot run it.", "Synthetic sine-wave few-shot regression (no download) is acceptable as the target task, run on CPU with the stated runtime budgets; fixed seeds make arm comparisons clean.", "Default hyperparameters (outer Adam lr, inner SGD lr, meta-batch, K, step counts) are fixed assumptions; any tuning is reported as sensitivity analysis."], "uncertainties": ["Exact TorchOpt version API details (location of the CG/solver handle, exact keyword names for `custom_root` and `implicit` modes, MetaOptimizer implicit flag) cannot be verified offline and depend on the pinned version; Step-0 docstring checks are mandatory before any run.", "Torch wheel availability for Python 3.14 at execution time is unknown, hence Experiment B is constrained to CPython 3.9-3.12 runtimes.", "Optimal inner-loop K and inner learning rate for satisfying the convergence/residual criterion require the pilot run to determine; defaults are provisional.", "Gradient-agreement and residual tolerance targets need pilot tuning and are reported as diagnostic curves rather than fixed pass/fail claims.", "Whether the runtime owner provides a TorchOpt checkout/install at execution (task.json declares repositories/compute not required) determines whether Experiment B can run at all; otherwise only Experiment A executes and B remains specified-only."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-76f8951d8b4c4433ab1813e53ed8b074", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-76f8951d8b4c4433ab1813e53ed8b074", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"stage\": \"result\", \"summary\": \"Final experiment design for implicit-diff meta-learning via TorchOpt\", \"answer\": \"FINAL EXPERIMENT DESIGN \\u2014 \\\"How can you implement implicit differentiation for meta-learning using TorchOpt?\\\"\\n\\n=====================================================================\\n0. DESIGN-ONLY STATUS AND GROUNDING\\n=====================================================================\\nThis is a design-only deliverable (task.json: \\\"repositories and compute are not required\\\"; no experiment was run). Inspection confirmed /workspace contains only task.json; no TorchOpt repo, no torch/torchopt/numpy/jax anywhere on disk; Python 3.14.4; network disabled. The design is therefore grounded in TorchOpt's DOCUMENTED public API (flagged \\\"not on-disk-verified\\\"), with:\\n  \\u2022 Step 0 (pinning) built into every script: print `torchopt.__version__` and the docstrings/signatures of `torchopt.diff.implicit.custom_root`, `torchopt.diff.implicit.diff`, `torchopt.F.module`, `torchopt.MetaOptimizer` and assert assumptions about keyword names before running; any mismatch is recorded, not silently worked around.\\n  \\u2022 An executable-in-this-sandbox, stdlib-only sanity check (Experiment A) that demonstrates the mathematics the TorchOpt API encodes, WITHOUT claiming the torch/torchopt pipeline ran.\\n  \\u2022 A fully specified torch/torchopt experiment (Experiment B) marked NOT-executable here.\\n\\n=====================================================================\\n1. RATIONALE & RESEARCH QUESTION\\n=====================================================================\\nQuestion: how to implement implicit differentiation for meta-learning using TorchOpt? Answer = (method recipe) + (validation design).\\n\\nFormalization (bi-level meta-learning):\\n  Outer:   min_\\u03b8  E_task ~ p(T) [ \\u2112_out( \\u03c6*(\\u03b8; T) ; D^val_T ) ]\\n  Inner:   \\u03c6*(\\u03b8;T) = argmin_\\u03c6 \\u2112_in( \\u03c6 ; \\u03b8, D^tr_T )   \\u21d4   g(\\u03c6,\\u03b8)=\\u2207_\\u03c6 \\u2112_in(\\u03c6; \\u03b8, D^tr_T)=0  (stationarity)\\n\\nTwo ways to get the outer gradient d\\u2112_out/d\\u03b8:\\n  \\u2022 Unrolled (MAML): backprop through the K inner steps \\u2014 memory/time grow with K.\\n  \\u2022 Implicit (iMAML, Rajeswaran et al. 2019): at a CONVERGED inner optimum, by the implicit function theorem,\\n        d\\u03c6*/d\\u03b8 = \\u2212[\\u2207\\u00b2_\\u03c6 \\u2112_in]^{-1} \\u00b7 \\u2207_\\u03b8\\u2207_\\u03c6 \\u2112_in\\n        d\\u2112_out/d\\u03b8 = \\u2212(\\u2207_\\u03c6 \\u2112_out)^T \\u00b7 [\\u2207\\u00b2_\\u03c6 \\u2112_in]^{-1} \\u00b7 \\u2207_\\u03b8\\u2207_\\u03c6 \\u2112_in   (solved via inverse-HVP linear solve, e.g. CG; O(1) in K, needs only first-order HVPs).\\n    This is precisely the backward TorchOpt's `diff.implicit` machinery computes: forward = solve to the inner fixed point, backward = linear solve + mixed-partial VJP.\\n\\nDesign answer structure:\\n  (A) Recipe using TorchOpt, two routes; (B) validation: stdlib-only IFT check (executable) + torch-based experiment with controls/metrics (specified).\\n\\n=====================================================================\\n2. METHOD \\u2014 TORCHOPT RECIPE (two routes)\\n=====================================================================\\nShared skeleton (functional model + inner loop identical across routes and controls):\\n```\\nimport torch, torch.nn.functional as F, torchopt\\nfrom torchopt import nn as tno\\n\\nclass SineMLP(tno.Functional):          # functional module, params-first\\n    def __init__(self):\\n        super().__init__(); self.fc1=tno.Linear(1,40); self.fc2=tno.Linear(40,40); self.fc3=tno.Linear(40,1)\\n    def forward(self,x,params=None):\\n        if params is None: params=torchopt.extract_state_dict(self, namespace='flatten')\\n        h=F.relu(self.fc1(x,params['fc1'])); h=F.relu(self.fc2(h,params['fc2'])); return self.fc3(h,params['fc3'])\\n\\ndef inner_loss(phi, theta, xs, ys):      # \\u2112_in on support set; theta=meta params\\n    return F.mse_loss(SineMLP()(xs, phi), ys)   # phi is the current task params\\ndef outer_loss(phi, theta, xq, yq):      # \\u2112_out on query set\\n    return F.mse_loss(SineMLP()(xq, phi), yq)\\n```\\nROUTE 1 \\u2014 explicit, most instructive (this is the \\\"how to implement\\\" core):\\n```\\nfrom torchopt import diff\\nimplicit_diff = diff.implicit.custom_root(\\n    solve=<solver handle, e.g. CG provided by the installed torchopt/linear-solver>,  # pinned in Step 0\\n    argnums=0, has_aux=True, rtol=1e-6, atol=1e-8, maxiter=50)\\n# Decorate the inner fixed-point iteration so the root \\u03c6*(\\u03b8) is differentiated implicitly:\\n@implicit_diff\\ndef inner_fixed_point(phi, theta, xs, ys):     # returns updated phi (root: phi'-phi=0 at convergence)\\n    for _ in range(K):\\n        g = torch.autograd.grad(inner_loss(phi, theta, xs, ys), phi, create_graph=False)[0]\\n        with torch.no_grad(): phi = [p - inner_lr*gp for p,gp in zip(phi,g)]\\n    return phi, (residual_info,)               # has_aux returns diagnostics\\n# Outer step:\\nphi_star, (res,) = inner_fixed_point(meta_theta, meta_theta, x_tr, y_tr)   # forward solve\\nouter_loss_val = outer_loss(phi_star, meta_theta, x_qu, y_qu)\\nouter_loss_val.backward(); meta_opt.step()     # implicit backward flows via decorator\\n```\\nROUTE 2 \\u2014 high-level TorchOpt meta API:\\n```\\nnet = torchopt.F.module(SineMLP(), implicit=True)\\nmeta_opt = torchopt.MetaOptimizer(net, torch.optim.SGD, lr=inner_lr, implicit=True)  # subclass e.g. MetaSGD\\n# inner loop: net.zero_grad(); inner_loss.backward(); meta_opt.step()   \\u2192 implicit update, no unrolled graph\\n# outer loop: standard torch.optim.Adam over meta_theta\\n```\\nBoth routes are specified; Route 1 is the primary answer (maximum control over the root condition and solver), Route 2 demonstrates the convenience API. Exact keyword names (`implicit`, `solve`, `argnums`, `has_aux`) are pinned in Step 0.\\n\\nInner-loop convergence enforcement & diagnosis (Refinement 1) \\u2014 CRITICAL because IFT is only valid at a converged inner optimum:\\n  \\u2022 All routes use the SAME inner optimizer (SGD), SAME learning rate inner_lr, SAME K, SAME initialization \\u03c6\\u2080=\\u03b8 (copy) \\u2014 the only difference is how the outer gradient is computed, so the comparison is clean.\\n  \\u2022 Pre-run convergence check (pilot, 100 tasks): run inner loop and record per-step (a) update norm \\u2016\\u03c6\\u2096\\u2212\\u03c6\\u2096\\u208b\\u2081\\u2016 and (b) stationarity residual \\u2016\\u2207_\\u03c6 \\u2112_in(\\u03c6\\u2096)\\u2016 (normalized by initial residual). Increase K (or lower inner_lr) until, on \\u226595% of pilot tasks, \\u2016\\u2207_\\u03c6 \\u2112_in(\\u03c6_K)\\u2016/\\u2016\\u2207_\\u03c6 \\u2112_in(\\u03c6\\u2080)\\u2016 < 1e-3 (or update-norm < atol for the chosen solver). These K and inner_lr values are then FIXED and reused identically by unrolled/first-order/implicit routes.\\n  \\u2022 At runtime, the implicit route ALSO emits residual_info per task (aux output) and logs the residual distribution per outer iteration; any outer step where residual exceeds the threshold is flagged (counted and reported), never silently trusted.\\n  \\u2022 The `rtol/atol/maxiter` args of `custom_root`/solver govern the inner/backward solve tolerances and are reported, not hidden.\\n\\n=====================================================================\\n3. EXPERIMENT A \\u2014 EXECUTABLE NOW IN THIS SANDBOX (stdlib only)\\n=====================================================================\\nPurpose: demonstrate the mathematics TorchOpt's implicit diff encodes, with ZERO dependencies, in this sandbox (Python 3.14.4, no torch). Framed explicitly as a math/feasibility check, NOT evidence that the TorchOpt pipeline ran.\\nSpecification:\\n  \\u2022 Pick an analytic implicit function: y*(\\u03b8) = real root of g(y,\\u03b8)=y\\u00b3\\u2212\\u03b8=0 \\u21d2 y*=\\u03b8^{1/3}, analytic dy*/d\\u03b8 = 1/(3\\u03b8^{2/3}). (Also run the Rosenbrock-style 2D fixed point x* = \\u03c6(x*,\\u03b8) for a second case.)\\n  \\u2022 Hand-implement the IFT backward exactly as `custom_root` does: given the root g=0, compute dy*/d\\u03b8 = \\u2212[\\u2202g/\\u2202y]^{-1}\\u00b7\\u2202g/\\u2202\\u03b8 using finite-difference partial derivatives (no autograd available) and a tiny dense solve implemented in pure Python.\\n  \\u2022 Verify: (i) against the analytic derivative, and (ii) against central finite differences of the root y*(\\u03b8). Report relative error and absolute error at several \\u03b8 values.\\n  \\u2022 Output: a small table + assertion thresholds like rel-err < 1e-6. Run via `python3 experiment_A_ift.py` \\u2014 fully runnable here.\\nDeliverable form: a single stdlib script plus a report of pass/critical residuals. This is the only executed portion in this sandbox.\\n\\n=====================================================================\\n4. EXPERIMENT B \\u2014 SPECIFIED, REQUIRES torch + torchopt (NOT executable here)\\n=====================================================================\\nEnvironment requirements (explicit): CPython 3.9\\u20133.12 recommended (Python 3.14 likely lacks torch wheels), torch \\u2265 2.x (needs torch.func/functorch for HVPs TorchOpt's implicit backward uses), torchopt from the pinned version's docs (pip install torchopt or a TorchOpt checkout). Network needed at setup time only; offline afterwards.\\nData: fully synthetic sine-wave few-shot regression (Finn et al. 2017 style): each task = y = A\\u00b7sin(x\\u2212\\u03c6)+\\u03b5, A~U[0.1,5], \\u03c6~U[0,\\u03c0]; support = 10 x-points, query = 10; 1000 meta-train tasks + 100 held-out meta-test tasks (generated by fixed seed, no download \\u2014 offline-safe).\\n\\nModel: SineMLP above (\\u22483.3k params).\\n\\nExperimental arms (all share the identical inner loop fixed in the pilot):\\n  R1 = Explicit implicit (`diff.implicit.custom_root`), K from convergence pilot.\\n  R2 = High-level implicit (`F.module(implicit=True)` + `MetaOptimizer(implicit=True)`), same K.\\n  C1 = Unrolled MAML control: same inner loop, but `F.module(default)`/unrolled mode \\u2014 backprop through the K steps.\\n  C2 = First-order MAML control: same inner loop with stop-gradient (detach \\u03c6) \\u2014 isolates the value of the implicit term.\\n  C0 = Gradient-agreement diagnostic (see metrics).\\n\\nHyperparameters (fixed defaults; state as assumptions): outer Adam lr 1e-3, inner SGD lr 0.01, meta-batch 8 tasks/step, 20k\\u201350k outer steps for full run (short run 2k steps for the pilot), K tuned in pilot (default 5, raised until residual threshold met), reproducibility: fixed seeds (python/torch/numpy), single config-file defining all arms.\\n\\nMetrics & analysis plan:\\n  M1 Gradient-agreement diagnostic (Refinement 3, framed as a SANITY CHECK not a pass/fail claim): on one small task, compute g_imp (R1), g_unroll (C1), g_fo (C2), and g_fd (central finite differences; feasible at this scale). Report cosine similarity cos=\\u27e8g_imp,g_unroll\\u27e9/(\\u2016\\u00b7\\u2016\\u2016\\u00b7\\u2016) and relative L2 error \\u2016g_imp\\u2212g_unroll\\u2016/\\u2016g_unroll\\u2016. Expected at converged inner loop: cos>0.999, rel-err<1e-3. If not met, report agreement as a function of K and of residual (the diagnostic distinguishes \\\"insufficient inner convergence\\\" from \\\"implementation bug\\\"); no binary claim is made, and the tuning/tolerance path (e.g. raise K, lower inner_lr, loosen/ tighten solver rtol/atol/maxiter) is reported alongside the numbers.\\n  M2 Meta-learning quality: outer-loss curves (smoothed), final few-shot test MSE = mean\\u00b1std over the 100 held-out tasks (R1 vs R2 vs C1 vs C2). Report per-arm and paired differences with error bars across 3 seeds.\\n  M3 Efficiency (Refinements 2): per-outer-step wall-clock time, peak memory (torch.cuda/psutil; CPU-only here), AND for the implicit arms the linear-solve cost: number of CG (or chosen solver) iterations per outer step, final residual norm of the inverse-HVP solve, and the solver's rtol/atol/maxiter. Plus the key K-sensitivity claim, explicitly framed as a claim to be tested: sweep K\\u2208{1,5,10,20} and show implicit cost ~flat in K while unrolled cost grows ~linearly \\u2014 report the fitted slope.\\nAnalysis plan summary: (1) run pilot (convergence/agreement/gradient curves), (2) fix K/lr from pilot, (3) run all arms with identical budgets, (4) produce tables: gradient agreement, final MSE, cost-per-step, CG stats, K-sensitivity; (5) report residuals/flag rates for the implicit arms alongside results.\\n\\n=====================================================================\\n5. FEASIBILITY & EXECUTION-SCOPE SEPARATION (Refinement 5)\\n=====================================================================\\n  \\u2022 Runs NOW in this sandbox (stdlib only): Experiment A (pure-Python IFT math check) \\u2014 the guaranteed-executed portion.\\n  \\u2022 SPECIFIED but NOT executable here (requires torch+torchopt env with network at setup): Experiment B (all arms, metrics, analysis) \\u2014 produced as code skeleton + config + run instructions; no executed results claimed for it.\\n  \\u2022 Runtime budget assumption (CPU-only, 224 cores available in sandbox, no GPU): pilot \\u2248 5\\u201315 min; short run \\u2248 30\\u201360 min; full run + K-sweep \\u2248 2\\u20134 h on a single CPU node. This is an explicit assumption, not a requirement, since the sandbox cannot run torch.\\n  \\u2022 Step-0 pinning is part of Experiment B's precondition: verify torchopt version and docstrings before launch; abort-and-report on signature mismatch rather than guessing.\\n\\n=====================================================================\\n6. ASSUMPTIONS\\n=====================================================================\\n  - TorchOpt's documented public API (custom_root, diff.implicit, F.module, MetaOptimizer(...,implicit), nn.Functional, extract_state_dict) is authoritative for the method since no on-disk repo/docs exist here and network is disabled; exact signatures pinned at execution.\\n  - Implicit differentiation is applied to the OUTER gradient of a bi-level meta-learning objective (iMAML-style); inner loop is ordinary functional SGD, shared verbatim across arms/controls.\\n  - The IFT is only used after the inner-loop convergence/residual criteria from the pilot are met; flagged tasks are never silently trusted.\\n  - A torch+torchopt environment (CPython 3.9\\u20133.12, torch\\u22652.x) and network-at-setup are provided at execution time; Python 3.14.4 in this sandbox cannot host them.\\n  - Synthetic sine-wave data (no download), CPU-only, and the stated runtime budgets are acceptable for the torch-based run.\\n  - Fixed seeds and identical hyperparameters across arms make the comparison clean; the design fixes defaults and reports any tuning as sensitivity analysis, not ad-hoc changes.\\n\\n=====================================================================\\n7. UNCERTAINTIES\\n=====================================================================\\n  - Exact TorchOpt version API (solver handle location, `implicit` keyword placement, `custom_root` signature) unverifiable offline \\u2192 Step-0 pinning; design includes fallback naming in the skeleton.\\n  - Python 3.14 wheel availability for torch/torchopt at execution time is unknown \\u2192 Experiment B constrained to 3.9\\u20133.12.\\n  - Optimal K / inner_lr for the residual criterion requires the pilot; defaults given.\\n  - Gradient-agreement tolerance targets need pilot tuning \\u2192 reported as diagnostic curves, not binary claims.\\n  - Whether the runtime owner chooses to supply a TorchOpt checkout at execution (task.json says not required) \\u2192 if not, only Experiment A executes; Experiment B remains fully specified-but-unexecuted.\\n\\nEvidence: exploration results above (ls/cat/find/python checks) confirm: empty /workspace except task.json; task.json declares design-only; no torchopt source, wheel, git repo, docs, or ML packages anywhere; python 3.14.4; no network/pip env. These are the grounded observations behind the design's grounding strategy and execution-scope separation.\", \"evidence\": \"Exploratory checks run in the sandbox (all successful, recorded in prior turns): (1) `ls -la /workspace` \\u2192 only task.json; (2) `cat /workspace/task.json` \\u2192 documents.track.md = \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"; (3) `find / -iname '*torchopt*'` \\u2192 empty; archive find \\u2192 no torchopt wheel/repo, only system archives; no `.git` dirs; no project `.md/.rst`; (4) `python3 -c 'import torch'` \\u2192 ModuleNotFoundError; importlib.find_spec for numpy/scipy/matplotlib/pandas/jax/functorch/optax all False; (5) python3 = 3.14.4; pip list shows only system packages; no pip index/proxy env vars. These confirm no repo/source/docs/deps/network, grounding the design in documented API with Step-0 pinning, a stdlib-only executable check (Experiment A), and a specified-only torch experiment (Experiment B).\", \"assumptions\": [\"TorchOpt's documented public API is authoritative for the method in the absence of on-disk repo/docs and network; exact signatures are pinned against the installed version's docstrings at execution (Step 0).\", \"Implicit differentiation is used for the OUTER/meta gradient of a bi-level meta-learning objective (iMAML-style); inner loop is ordinary functional SGD shared verbatim across all arms and controls.\", \"The implicit-function-theorem gradient is only trusted after inner-loop convergence/residual criteria from a pilot are met; non-converged tasks are flagged and reported, never silently included.\", \"Execution of the torch/torchopt experiment requires a separate environment: CPython 3.9\\u20133.12, torch >= 2.x, and torchopt installed (network at setup only); this sandbox (Python 3.14.4, no torch, no network) cannot run it.\", \"Synthetic sine-wave few-shot regression (no download) is acceptable as the target task, run on CPU with the stated runtime budgets; fixed seeds make arm comparisons clean.\", \"Default hyperparameters (outer Adam lr, inner SGD lr, meta-batch, K, step counts) are fixed assumptions; any tuning is reported as sensitivity analysis.\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"Exact TorchOpt version API details (location of the CG/solver handle, exact keyword names for `custom_root` and `implicit` modes, MetaOptimizer implicit flag) cannot be verified offline and depend on the pinned version; Step-0 docstring checks are mandatory before any run.\", \"Torch wheel availability for Python 3.14 at execution time is unknown, hence Experiment B is constrained to CPython 3.9-3.12 runtimes.\", \"Optimal inner-loop K and inner learning rate for satisfying the convergence/residual criterion require the pilot run to determine; defaults are provisional.\", \"Gradient-agreement and residual tolerance targets need pilot tuning and are reported as diagnostic curves rather than fixed pass/fail claims.\", \"Whether the runtime owner provides a TorchOpt checkout/install at execution (task.json declares repositories/compute not required) determines whether Experiment B can run at all; otherwise only Experiment A executes and B remains specified-only.\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-f5c1bbd80c3b483794261670b166bca5", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Final experiment design for implicit-diff meta-learning via TorchOpt", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
